fact WorkItem(id: "WI-809", description: some(value: "A named-argument list may not REPEAT A LABEL, at any callee. MEASURED on a WELL-FORMED entity (distinct fields, so WI-806's declaration rule does not apply): `entity mk(a: Int64, b: Int64)` with `mk(a: 1, a: 2).a` loads with ZERO errors and returns Int(1). The value built has TWO `a` fields and NO `b` -- `.a` reads the first, a positional pattern sees the second `a` occupying b's slot, and `.b` raises Internal(\"field_access: entity has no field 'b'\") at RUN TIME. So one label written twice silently fills an unrelated field and leaves the intended one unbound. THE CHECK ALREADY EXISTED AND ENTITIES DID NOT USE IT: the identical spelling against an OPERATION was refused by named_arg_coverage_errors ('binds a parameter already given'); entity construction, facts and rule-body atoms never route through it. FIX AT THE SYNTAX LAYER, not by teaching the typer a fourth callee shape: whether one list repeats a label needs NO type information, so one rule covers every callee at once (check_label_unique with a NAMED_ARGS_DISTINCT rationale). named_arg_coverage_errors KEEPS both of its own reasons, neither syntactic -- an UNKNOWN label, and one colliding with a parameter already filled POSITIONALLY (`f(3, acc: 10)`, WI-783). ACCEPTANCE: the program above is refused at parse with a located diagnostic naming the label; the same refusal for a fact, a rule-body atom, an operation call and a dot call; distinct labels still construct and read; spec states the rule alongside 4.5 and 6.3; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-22T05:07:13Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-809", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. Full workspace 3372 passed / 0 failed.\n\nFOUR PRODUCTIONS, NOT TWO — and the /code-review pass is what found the other two, for the THIRD ticket running. My first cut patched push_fn_term and push_dot_method_call and I wrote that it 'covers every callee at once'. The `named_arg` grammar node actually reaches THREE productions (fn_term, tuple_literal, proof_strategy) plus the pattern twin `named_pattern_field`:\n\n(a) PROOF STRATEGY args were uncovered and the gap was self-undermining: `by z3(logic: \\\"LRA\\\", logic: \\\"QF_NRA\\\")` parsed CLEAN while the NESTED spelling `z3(tactic: smt(logic: .., logic: ..))` was refused — because the nested value routes through convert_term into push_fn_term. prove.rs reads these LAST-WINS and pushes BOTH into the proof-cache canon, so the duplicate silently changed what was proved AND what was cached. Driven both ways.\n\n(b) CONSTRUCTOR PATTERNS: I had excluded them claiming they were 'already loud at run time' via the WI-445 double-cover guard. I MEASURED THAT ON THE WRONG FIXTURE. It raises MatchFailed only with NO following case; with a fallback — the common shape — the malformed arm just never matches: `case mk(a: p, a: q) -> 111` under `case _ -> 999` loaded clean and returned 999. A SILENTLY DEAD ARM, the exact failure mode this codebase ranks below a loud error. My own test file asserted the false claim.\n\nI FABRICATED A JUSTIFICATION AND IT WAS CAUGHT. When WI-807 subsumed WI-805's variadic-capture guard, I kept the guard and justified it by saying a WI-722 compile-time macro could synthesize an Expr::Apply with named args and bypass the parser. FALSE, verified in code: reflect_make_apply and reflect_make_fn both hardcode an empty named-arg list, and try_expand_macro DECLINES any template carrying one; the only synthesizer that does carry them (substitute_to_occurrence) copies keys from a source-parsed Term::Fn. The guard is retained as defence-in-depth with NO KNOWN REACHABLE PATH, and now says exactly that. A rationale that sounds reachable is worse than none — it stops the next reader re-checking. Same class of error as the WI-806 arrow-parameter rationale I got wrong yesterday.\n\nALSO: I referenced a function `push_dot_apply` in FOUR places (CLAUDE.md, typing.rs, two test sites). It does not exist — the function is push_dot_method_call. Corrected.\n\nWHY THE SYNTAX LAYER, and the split that survives it: repetition WITHIN one list needs no type information, so one rule covers every callee. named_arg_coverage_errors keeps both of ITS reasons, neither decidable from the list alone — an UNKNOWN label, and one re-binding a parameter already filled POSITIONALLY. Verified the positional case is still reachable on the OPERATION path too (`take(3, a: 10)` -> 'binds a parameter already given'), not just WI-783's function-value twin, so the typer check is not orphaned.\n\nOVER-FIRE CHECKED HARDEST ON RULE BODIES, since `mk(a: ?x, a: ?y)` could plausibly have meant 'unify this field with two patterns' — a real logic-programming reading. It never did: BuildFrame::FnTerm pushes each named slot verbatim into Term::Fn.named_args, no conjunction anywhere, so such an atom could only ever match an equally malformed fact. The intended meaning stays expressible as two goals. Per-list scoping verified: separate calls and nested calls may reuse a label.\n\nCONTROL-TESTED per site: disabling both call-site guards fails 5 tests; disabling ONLY push_fn_term leaves the dot-call test passing (proving that second site is independently witnessed — it was the one that would have stayed silently open); disabling the two new guards fails exactly their 2 tests.\n\nDocs: kernel-language.md states the rule in the named-arguments section and narrows the function-value note (an unknown label is still a load error, a duplicate is refused earlier); CLAUDE.md records the syntax-vs-semantic split.", at: "2026-07-22T05:07:43Z")
